Title: re-enable alfe --mode automation on macOS and debug osascript driving of BricsCAD
Status: OPEN — injection layer FIXED + verified live (Round 6, 2026-09-11);
  blocked on a separate, deeper protocol-loop bug, see
  issues/open/alfe-bricscad-automation-macos-stopped-status.issue
Priority: P3
Type: feature / investigation
Depends: issues/closed/alfe-bricscad-automation-macos.issue

* What

Pursue OPTION B of the (now closed) alfe-bricscad-automation-macos
ticket: keep --mode automation refused by DEFAULT on macOS, but make it
possible to RE-ENABLE it explicitly, and then debug the osascript /
AppleScript keystroke path against a real BricsCAD, interactively, at the
machine. pjb, 2026-09-11: "re-enable --mode automation and debug osa
scripting with bricscad on macos. We'll do it on macos."

This is NOT a CI task and NOT reproducible headless. It needs an
interactive GUI session with BricsCAD visible, Accessibility (TCC)
granted to the shell running osascript, and a human watching where the
injected text lands.

* Background (see the closed ticket for the full five-round history)

On macOS, --mode automation drives BricsCAD with osascript running the
emitted launcher.applescript (EMIT-LAUNCHER-APPLESCRIPT), which injects
`(load "run-common.lsp")' via System Events keystrokes. On 2026-08-14 pjb
took OPTION A: --mode automation is refused on every non-Windows OS
(BACKEND-NOT-AVAILABLE :NO-AUTOMATION), so it fails immediately instead of
launching a CAD and timing out. That refusal is what the CI job
verify:bricscad-automation:macos hits today (job 16444148404: phase 1
automation FAIL exit=3 [NO-AUTOMATION], phase 2 batch PASS — the runner
and BricsCAD are healthy; the mode is simply disabled).

Everything needed to investigate was deliberately KEPT: the AppleScript
emitter, MACOS-APP-BUNDLE-FOR, the Accessibility preflight, the
launcher-state / launcher-focus reporting, the LAUNCHER-FAILED fast-fail,
and scripts/verify-bricscad-automation.sh with its batch control phase.

Five hypotheses were already eliminated on the real runner (script
validity, Accessibility/TCC, launcher liveness, application focus,
missing document). What remains — all GUI-level, machine-only:

  - keyboard focus INSIDE BricsCAD is not on the command line (keystrokes
    reach the app but land in the drawing area / a ribbon search box /
    the Start tab);
  - a modal or first-run dialog owns input;
  - BricsCAD on macOS does not accept command input via synthetic
    CGEvents at all — it may read the keyboard through a path that
    `System Events' keystroke does not drive.

* Plan

1. OPT-IN RE-ENABLE (does not change the default). In
   CHOOSE-EFFECTIVE-MODE (autolisp-front-end/source/backend-bricscad.lisp,
   ~line 767) the refusal `(when (and (eq variant :automation) (not
   (windows-p))) (error ...))' becomes conditional on an explicit opt-in
   — e.g. an env var ALFE_ENABLE_MACOS_AUTOMATION=1 (and/or a hidden CLI
   flag). Without the opt-in, macOS behaves EXACTLY as today (immediate
   :NO-AUTOMATION, --mode batch the supported path). The BUILD-LAUNCH-ARGV
   :automation macOS branch (~line 823) already emits `osascript
   launcher.applescript', so nothing else is needed to reach the path.

2. DEBUG AT THE MACHINE (option B). With the opt-in set, run
   scripts/verify-bricscad-automation.sh (or the emitted
   launcher.applescript by hand) with BricsCAD visible and watch where
   the text goes. protocol/status.txt, protocol/stdout.txt,
   launcher-focus.txt and the kept workdir are the instruments.

3. HARDEN THE INJECTION — the concrete things to try, roughly in order of
   promise (the unsolved failure is: keystrokes reach a FOCUSED BricsCAD
   and still do not execute — so the `keystroke' mechanism itself is the
   prime suspect, not timing):
   - CLIPBOARD PASTE instead of literal keystroke: `set the clipboard to
     "(load \"...\")"', `keystroke "v" using command down', then
     `keystroke return'. This is the leading candidate for why per-char
     `keystroke' never took effect.
   - DELIVERY CONFIRMATION LOOP: after injecting, poll protocol/status.txt
     for READY -> request -> DONE; if no progress within N s, re-`activate'
     BricsCAD and re-inject, bounded to a few attempts, so a lost inject
     is retried rather than silently timing out.
   - EXPLICIT ACTIVATE + RE-ASSERT FRONTMOST immediately before each
     burst (not only once at startup), and consider clicking/placing the
     command line via UI scripting (AXUIElement) so focus is on the input
     field, addressing the top remaining hypothesis.
   - If synthetic CGEvents are simply not accepted: evaluate a non-keystroke
     channel (e.g. a BricsCAD-side listener that reads a file/socket, the
     way the batch file-protocol already works), and treat AppleScript as
     launch-only.

* Acceptance

- With the opt-in set, verify:bricscad-automation:macos phase 1 completes
  the protocol cycle on a real macOS + BricsCAD GUI session (READY ->
  request -> DONE, marker echoed), OR the artefacts pin down which of the
  remaining hypotheses is the cause.
- The DEFAULT is unchanged: without the opt-in, macOS still refuses
  --mode automation immediately (the existing tests in
  backend-cad-tests.lisp must stay green — assert on both platforms so
  they are not vacuous on the Windows lane).
- Once a working path exists, decide whether to promote it from opt-in to
  default on macOS, or keep it opt-in with --mode batch remaining the
  documented macOS path.

* Notes / constraints

- Cannot be verified in CI or by an agent without the machine: no
  interactive GUI, no Accessibility grant, no human to observe. This
  ticket is a human-at-the-keyboard task on macOS.
- Keep the change minimal and reversible: the opt-in must not weaken the
  immediate, honest failure that option A introduced for the common
  "no BricsCAD installed" case.

* Round 6 — live at the machine, 2026-09-11: injection FIXED, one layer
  deeper opened

pjb + Claude, live BricsCAD V26 on macOS (this machine), Accessibility
granted to the invoking process (the nested claude-code helper .app needed
LSREGISTER first — Finder's "+" picker silently refuses an unregistered
bundle; `lsregister -f <bundle>` fixes it).

** Implemented (autolisp-front-end/source/backend-bricscad.lisp)

  - MACOS-AUTOMATION-OPT-IN-P + CHOOSE-EFFECTIVE-MODE: the refusal is now
    conditional on $ALFE_ENABLE_MACOS_AUTOMATION (unset = exactly the old
    behaviour; the existing off-by-default tests stay green unmodified).
  - *BRICSCAD-APPLESCRIPT-TEMPLATE* hardened:
    - Targets the console window by its AXMain=true attribute, NOT by
      title (titles are translated — "Historique des invites" in French,
      "Prompt History" in English — matching by name would break on any
      non-English install).
    - Clicks into that window before typing. This is the actual fix:
      "BricsCAD is frontmost" was already true in every earlier CI round
      (launcher-focus.txt said so) and was STILL not enough, because
      BricsCAD Lite runs its console as a window separate from the
      drawing window — frontmost-app-level checks cannot tell which of
      the app's OWN windows has keyboard focus.
    - Types in 8-character bursts, re-checking frontmost after each, so a
      mid-injection deviation (another window stealing focus) is
      detected instead of silently swallowed.
    - On deviation: Escape (discard whatever partial fragment landed),
      `display dialog` asking the user to stop interfering, retry up to
      3 times. Honest limitation, stated in the template's own comments:
      the console exposes NO accessible text value anywhere in its AX
      tree (verified — see below), so there is no way to read back how
      much of a partial injection landed. The retry therefore always
      redoes the WHOLE command rather than attempting a character-level
      resume, which would need state that provably does not exist.

** Verified live (not merely osacompile-clean)

  (+ 1 2) typed into the real console -> 3, evaluated and echoed.
  (load "<fake path>") -> BricsCAD's own "LOAD failed" error, i.e. the
  CAD genuinely parsed and ran the injected form.
  The real production payload, (load "<run-common.lsp>"), typed via the
  ACTUAL emitted launcher.applescript (byte-identical to what
  EMIT-LAUNCHER-APPLESCRIPT produces — extracted from a real `alfe
  --bricscad --mode automation --print-command` run, not hand-transcribed)
  -> BricsCAD evaluated it and returned nil, THREE separate times across
  three separate runs (one manual, two via scripts/verify-bricscad-
  automation.sh phase 1). This closes out all three remaining hypotheses
  from the earlier rounds:
    - "keyboard focus lands elsewhere" -> CONFIRMED as the cause, and
      FIXED (window-targeted click).
    - "a modal/first-run dialog owns input" -> none was present; two
      tiny AXDialog windows seen during probing were unrelated
      Screen-Recording-permission-overlay cruft, not BricsCAD's.
    - "BricsCAD does not accept synthetic CGEvents at all" -> FALSIFIED;
      it does, reliably, once the right window is focused.
  One real bug was caught and fixed only by actually running this (not
  by osacompile syntax-checking alone): a UI-element reference returned
  out of a `tell' block does not resolve on its own outside that
  context (macOS -1700) — FOCUSCONSOLEWINDOW's `position of cmdWin' /
  `size of cmdWin' needed their own `tell application \"System Events\"'.
  A second regression was caught by the EXISTING test suite: the
  deviation-retry's re-activation line unconditionally used `open -a',
  which broke MACOS-LAUNCHER-APPLESCRIPT-FALLS-BACK-TO-PLAIN-BINARY
  (asserts `open -a' never appears for a non-bundled bricscad) — fixed
  by reactivating via System Events' `set frontmost of process' instead,
  which works either way and does not risk reopening docPath as a new
  document.

** NOT yet exercised live: the deviation/dialog/retry path itself. It is
  osacompile-clean and logic-reviewed, but reliably TRIGGERING a
  mid-burst focus change from a second concurrent actor was not attempted
  this round — the happy path already needed the machine time. Worth a
  follow-up manual pass: run the launcher, then deliberately click another
  app mid-injection, and confirm the dialog appears and the retry lands
  the command correctly.

** New, DEEPER, SEPARATE problem found: verify:bricscad-automation:macos
  phase 1 still does not reach the full READY -> request -> DONE cycle,
  even now that keystrokes land correctly. Two live end-to-end runs (one
  with ALFE_PROBE_TIMEOUT=60, one at the script's real 240s default) both
  ended with protocol/status.txt = STOPPED, marker-present=0, top-level
  status.txt = \"99\" (a value the runtime source already calls \"the
  mystery status 99\" in its own comments — autolisp-remote-io.lsp, two
  occurrences). Diagnostics prove the CAD-side runtime genuinely started:
  protocol/heartbeat.txt, runtime-flags.txt and runtime-info.txt are all
  populated (BricsCAD / 26.0 BricsCAD / BRICSCAD), and the live console
  showed `(load \"…run-common.lsp\")' -> nil with no CAD-side error. So
  AUTOLISP-PROTOCOL-SERVER-LOOP reached its `(autolisp-protocol-set-status
  \"READY 0\")' at minimum. Ruled out: AUTOLISP-PROTOCOL-REMOTE-READ-LINE
  is purely file-poll based (STDINFILE + control.txt), not raw process
  stdin, so a detached `open -a'-launched BricsCAD having no piped stdin
  is NOT the cause (that was my first hypothesis; checking the source
  disproved it). The loop only reaches its terminal
  `(autolisp-protocol-set-status \"STOPPED\")' via its normal fall-through
  (the `while (not *AUTOLISP_PROTOCOL_STOP*)' exiting) — i.e. something
  set *AUTOLISP_PROTOCOL_STOP*, most likely a SHUTDOWN line arriving via
  protocol/control.txt. Leading hypothesis, NOT yet confirmed: alfe's own
  client-side READY-wait or session-teardown path writes SHUTDOWN to
  control.txt on some condition that fires for the automation variant but
  not batch — worth checking WAIT-FOR-STATUS-PREFIX / whatever writes
  control.txt from the alfe side next. This is out of scope for THIS
  ticket (which was specifically about the injection layer, now fixed and
  proven) — filed as issues/open/alfe-bricscad-automation-macos-stopped-status.issue.

